WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.767
-Thank you so much
for coming back to the show,

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.033
and thanks for playing
"Random Instrument Challenge."

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:06.334
-That was wild!
-Yeah. You know what's crazier?

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:09.467
So, during the break, you told
one of our segment producers

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:10.634
that you actually know
how to play

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:12.734
one of the weird instruments
we had there.

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:15.367
-Yeah, about as well
as I played the sousaphone

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:18.100
and the upright bass.
-No, but I was in shock.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.000
I go, "Wait. This is --
We have to --"

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.734
I want to see you play this,

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:24.367
because I know
kind of the idea behind it.

00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:26.200
This is a theremin.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.934
So, this is pitch.

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:34.200
[ Theremin plays ]

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.868
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:44.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:45.934
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.367
-Oh, my God!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.300
Wow! Maggie Gyllenhaal!
I mean, that is --

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.400
that is a talent right there.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.801
-Yeah, this is, like,
the extent of my musical talent.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:01.801
-I love that.
Well, last time you were here,

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.267
speaking of talents,
we were talking about

00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:07.267
"The Lost Daughter," but how
you adapted it from this book

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.934
that you read by the same name.
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:11.467
-You've already won
and been nominated

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:15.133
for all these awards,
and I think totally deserved.

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:18.501
It was a beautiful movie.
It was gorgeous.

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:20.667
In fact,
at Venice Film Festival,

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:22.067
that's where, I think,
we first heard about it.

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:24.667
It got like a 20-minute
standing ovation or something.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:28.000
Crazy. Were you nervous even
just going into the festival?

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.868
-Yeah, I mean, look,
we made the film in a pandemic.

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:34.300
But more than that,
we cut it in a pandemic,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.033
before there were
any vaccines or anything.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.367
I was cutting
this time last year.

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:43.300
And so we never showed it
to an audience, ever.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.234
Which, if you don't make films,
I don't know,

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:47.667
it may be hard to know
how strange that is.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.934
But, usually, you invite
your friends and family,

00:01:49.934 --> 00:01:51.968
you invite, you know --
-Screen it and --

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.701
-Then you screen it,
and you find out how it plays,

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:56.167
and you hear people laugh,
and then people give you notes,

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:59.567
and you adjust the film
sometimes based on those notes.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.634
Here it was just
me and my editor.

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.100
I mean,
we showed to it a few people,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.400
but they were people
who already loved us.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.534
You know? They were --
-Yeah, it's like my mom

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:09.667
going like, "Jimmy,
you were the greatest person

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:11.701
ever to be on
'Saturday Night Live.'"

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.801
I go, "Oh, my God.
What a great review, Mom."

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:14.968
-Exactly.
-"Thanks."

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:17.667
-Exactly.
And so the first time

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:22.367
I ever saw my film
with any audience --

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:25.634
like, I'm talking anything --
was at Venice.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:28.968
And the Grande Sala
with hundreds of people

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:31.334
and Olivia Colman
and Dakota were there --

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:34.534
Dakota Johnson -- and they had
never seen the movie at all.

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:37.901
Sitting next to me
at the Venice Film Festival

00:02:37.901 --> 00:02:41.200
was the first time.
-How do you set it up,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.567
and how do you talk about
what the movie's about?

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:48.267
-Well, it's about a woman
who goes on vacation alone,

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:53.000
and while she's on vacation,
she gets really compelled

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.334
by this young mother
that she sees on the beach,

00:02:55.334 --> 00:02:58.634
and it starts bringing up
for her all of these

00:02:58.634 --> 00:03:02.501
wild, dark, strange memories
in her own life

00:03:02.501 --> 00:03:04.367
that start
to really trip her out.

00:03:04.367 --> 00:03:06.501
-Because she was a young mother,
and...

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:08.067
I don't want to spoil anything,
but, uh...

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:10.434
-She was a young mother
who struggled

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:13.667
with being a mother, like,
if we're honest,

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:16.367
I think every mother
who ever existed.

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:20.100
-Yeah.
-But, you know, I think

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.767
we're used to either seeing
a sort of fantasy version

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.467
of the mother
that we all wished we had

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.734
or the mother
that we wished we were,

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:33.200
or, like, a bad mommy.
You know?

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.367
And the thing that that does is,
it makes you go like,

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:38.234
"Oh, look at that
crazy, bad mommy.

00:03:38.234 --> 00:03:40.234
I'm nothing like that.
Thank God.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.868
You know, I'm such a good mama."
But to join them together,

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:45.033
which is what this movie does,

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:47.400
you know, where it's
complicated, you know?

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.167
-But it's so well done and shot
and directed and written.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.834
And you wrote
and directed this, buddy.

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:56.000
And I will say this --
last time you were here,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.067
we were talking about this.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.300
You were thinking about
optioning.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.467
And I go, "Why is it --

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:02.167
Why are you waiting this long
to direct?"

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:04.734
Do you remember
what you said to me?

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:07.934
-I think I said, um...

00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:12.834
that it took me a long time
to even allow myself

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.801
to feel how much
I wanted to do it,

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:20.901
that I didn't feel entitled
to even think about it.

00:04:20.901 --> 00:04:22.534
-Now that you did it,
do you feel great

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:24.634
that you're on the other side
and you now have a movie

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:27.100
under your belt
that's fantastic?

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.501
-I feel like...

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:33.267
I love acting,
and I miss acting,

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:36.534
but I was always, like,
bumping up against something --

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:39.501
you know? --
about what I wanted to say

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:41.934
or express or do or offer.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:45.734
And it was like --

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:47.501
I don't even know
how to explain it.

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:49.300
It was like --
Do you ever have a dream that --

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.334
It's like a common dream I think
people have where they're,

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:55.200
like, in their little apartment
and they dream that

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.567
they open a door,
and all of a sudden,

00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:59.701
there's an entire other wing?
You know?

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:02.200
And you're like --
You've had that dream.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.634
-Yeah.
[ Light laughter ]

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:07.133
I had a dream that I was getting
chased by a shark last night.

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:08.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:09.567
-But you've never
had that dream?

00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:11.234
This is what it was like.
It was like, all of a sudden,

00:05:11.234 --> 00:05:14.033
I just knocked a wall down,
and I was like,

00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:16.767
"It's so much better over here."
-Yeah.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:20.100
-Because I do think,
when women make movies,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.100
and women do a lot of things
and really express themselves,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.934
it looks different.

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:30.934
And something new and different
is really compelling, you know?

00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:33.133
And honest.
-I appreciate you being honest

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:35.501
even when we talked about
before you did this

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.601
and you were like,
"I don't know if I even know."

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:40.067
Couldn't even talk about it,
even directing it, I think,

00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:42.968
to now, you did it,
and it's a homer.

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:47.501
Honestly, it's moving,
it's beautiful, it's fantastic.

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:49.100
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.667
Here's a peek
at "The Lost Daughter."

00:05:50.667 --> 00:05:52.167
Take a look.

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:56.234
-[ Breathing heavily ]

00:05:56.234 --> 00:05:59.100
She's driving me crazy.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.834
'Cause Daddy's away, right?

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:05.868
Daddy's away,
and you're taking it out on me.

00:06:05.868 --> 00:06:07.634
-All right.
Come to auntie, baby.

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:08.634
Come on, come to auntie.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.033
There we go, my big girl.

00:06:11.033 --> 00:06:13.100
Oh, boy!
My big girl.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.234
-Go, so I can have a minute.

00:06:16.234 --> 00:06:19.734
Sometimes I just
can't handle it.

00:06:19.734 --> 00:06:21.634
-I know.

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:24.067
-Okay?
Yes, anything you want.

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:29.267
Show Mama -- Show Mama and the
lady what a good girl you are.

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:30.634
-Ooh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:33.234
Maggie Gyllenhaal, everybody.
"The Lost Daughter"

00:06:33.234 --> 00:06:36.133
is in select theaters
and streaming now on Netflix.

